        "summary":  "NVIDIA details its 800 VDC direct-current power architecture as the solution to the efficiency losses inherent in traditional AC power distribution at the densities required for next-generation AI computing, arguing that fewer AC-to-DC conversion stages mean more power reaches the accelerator. The strategy is designed for pragmatic adoption ΓÇö a hybrid-compatible power rack enables immediate use in existing data centers, with row-level and facility-scale rollouts planned through 2027. The approach is being developed as an open standard through the Open Compute Project with Google, Microsoft, and 80+ equipment manufacturers.",
        "execSummary":  "NVIDIA is standardizing on 800 VDC as the power architecture for AI factories, and the open-standard approach through the Open Compute Project means this is likely to become the industry baseline rather than a proprietary lock-in. For enterprise technology and facilities leaders planning AI infrastructure investments, the hybrid-compatible rack design is the key near-term decision point ΓÇö it allows higher compute density adoption without full data center redesigns, protecting existing capital expenditure. The projected $9 trillion in global AI infrastructure investment through 2040 cited in the article reflects the scale at which these infrastructure decisions will compound. The strategic caveat is that enterprises relying on traditional IT procurement cycles will fall behind those that build AI infrastructure planning into their capital strategy now.",
        "vendorLean":  "NVIDIA",
        "imageUrl":  "https://blogs.nvidia.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/800vdc-1280x720.jpg"
