        "title":  "Compassion is not a control: What veterinary practices reveal about AI governance",
        "source":  "CIO.com",
        "url":  "https://www.cio.com/article/4203552/compassion-is-not-a-control-what-veterinary-practices-reveal-about-ai-governance.html",
        "date":  "2026-08-18",
        "theme":  "Strategy",
        "summary":  "Using veterinary medicine as a lens, the article examines how well-intentioned teams in smaller clinical environments can inadvertently create AI risks without proper oversight structures. It argues that compassion and good intentions alone are insufficient governance substitutes, and advocates for lightweight but explicit frameworks covering approved tools, data protection, and clear accountability before AI is deployed. The piece generalizes this lesson to any organization adopting AI without formal governance scaffolding.",
        "execSummary":  "Even mission-driven teams with the best intentions can expose their organizations to serious AI risk when governance is left informal. The article\u0027s central lesson for enterprise leaders is that cultural goodwill is not a substitute for documented policies on approved AI tools, data handling, and accountability chains. For customer-support operations specifically, this means formalizing AI use policies before scaling pilots ΓÇö not after problems surface. The caveat is that governance frameworks must be proportionate and lightweight enough not to stifle adoption, requiring CIOs to balance oversight with operational agility.",
        "vendorLean":  "Neutral",
        "imageUrl":  null
